{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control or rent leveling ordinance for 30 years following completion of construction. Rents subsidized by government funding may also be exempt.","key_value":"30 years","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings in municipalities that have rent control or rent leveling ordinances; the exemption runs 30 years from completion of construction.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings exempt from local rent control for 30 years after completion of construction"},"exemptions":"Rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"Under the Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5), newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act rent increase notice and unconscionability rule","requirement":"A landlord cannot raise the rent mid-lease; the old lease must be terminated and the tenant offered a new lease at the increased rent by proper written notice. The increase must not be unconscionable.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants; an increase must not be so unreasonable as to shock the conscience of a fair and honest person, and must comply with municipal rent ordinances.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential leases; rent increase notice requirements"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot raise the rent mid-lease term. The old lease must be terminated, and the new lease must have the increased rental payment. The landlord has to offer the tenant a new lease with the increased rent once the old lease has been terminated.","interaction":"Rent increases must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Conversion cost rent increase prohibition","requirement":"Where a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion. Increases for new services or amenities are allowed.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in a building converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership of units, whether or not they qualify as senior citizens or disabled persons.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":"Increases to cover the cost of new services or amenities are not prohibited.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease only for good cause. Each cause except nonpayment of rent must be described in detail in a written Notice to Quit, and a court judgment is required before removal.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential dwelling units used as a residence. Applies to written and oral leases; a Notice to Cease may also be required before a Notice to Quit.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential dwelling units used as a residence"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit transfer, dispute and penalty rules","requirement":"When a rental property is sold, the new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit and accrued interest and is responsible for investing it, giving notices, paying interest and returning it. Unlawful use or willful withholding of deposits is penalized.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential rental security deposits. Disputes go to Small Claims up to $5,000 or the Special Civil Part up to $15,000; no state agency has jurisdiction.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential rental security deposits"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Willfully and intentionally withholding the deposit of a tenant receiving state or federal financial assistance: civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 per offense. Unlawful use of deposit monies: disorderly person, fine of not less than $200 or up to 30 days' imprisonment, or both.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"If the property is sold or transferred it is the duty of the new owner to obtain the security deposit, plus accrued interest on the tenant’s deposit, that was collected by the former owner.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Law Against Discrimination source-of-lawful-income protection","requirement":"A landlord or rental agency may not refuse to rent or discriminate in housing because of a renter's source of lawful income; the LAD requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships, except two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner or resident-occupied single home, and residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Excepts owner-occupied two-family dwellings and rooms in owner- or resident-occupied single homes"},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in an owner or resident-occupied single home; residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex (as to familial status); age-restricted housing.","penalty":"Landlords who violate the LAD are subject to substantial fines and penalties, up to $10,000 for a first offense.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
